      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2060516075851153914",
      "url": "https://x.com/GlobalCyclones/status/2060516075851153914",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/GlobalCyclones/status/2060516075851153914",
      "text": "🌀 Western Pacific Tropical Bulletin \n— TYPHOON #Jangmi (#DomengPH)\nIssued: Saturday May 30, 2026, 8: 00 AM Philippine Time\nAreas of Concern: Philippines, Taiwan, and \nthe Ryukyu Islands of Japan (especially Okinawa)\n\nJangmi, known in the Philippines as “Domeng,” continues to strengthen over the Philippine Sea and is now a TYPHOON tracking  northward toward the southern Japanese islands during the June 1–2, 2026, timeframe. Forecast guidance increasingly suggests the system could pass near or just west of Okinawa and portions of the Ryukyu Island chain before curving northeastward. \n\n🇵🇭 Philippines\nAlthough the core of Jangmi is expected to remain east of Luzon, the cyclone is already enhancing the southwest monsoon (Habagat) which may bring periods of heavy rainfall, gusty winds, and rough seas to western sections of Luzon and the Visayas through the weekend. Coastal communities and fishermen should closely monitor local advisories due to increasing wave heights and the potential for localized flooding. PAGASA and Philippine civil defense agencies have already upgraded alerts as the system intensifies. \n\n🇹🇼 Taiwan\nTaiwan is not currently expected to receive a direct landfall from Jangmi, but significant peripheral effects are possible between May 31 and June 2. Forecasters indicate strong winds, dangerous surf, and high waves affecting eastern, southeastern, and northern coastal waters, as well as the Taiwan Strait. Maritime conditions are expected to deteriorate considerably, and residents along exposed coastlines should remain alert for hazardous seas and squally weather bands. \n\n🇯🇵 Ryukyu Islands / Okinawa\nThe Ryukyu Islands of Japan appear to be the area facing the greatest potential impact from Jangmi. Current JTWC-style forecast guidance indicates closest approach near Okinawa around late June 1, 2026 into June 2, 2026, likely as a Category 2 equivalent TYPHOON with strong winds, torrential rainfall, dangerous surf, and possible coastal flooding. U.S. military installations on Okinawa have already entered elevated tropical readiness conditions as confidence increases in impacts across the island chain. \n\nResidents across Okinawa and neighboring islands should prepare for:\nDamaging tropical-storm-force to typhoon-force winds\nFlash flooding and landslides in mountainous terrain\nDangerous marine conditions and very rough surf\nPossible transportation disruptions and power outages\nForecast models currently suggest the storm may begin recurving northeastward after passing near the Ryukyus, but even a small track shift could significantly alter impacts across Okinawa, Amami, and adjacent islands. \n\n🌀 SUMMARY:\nJangmi is becoming a significant early-season TYPHOON threat for the Ryukyu Islands of Japan while also generating hazardous marine and monsoonal conditions for Taiwan and the Philippines. Interests across Okinawa and the broader Ryukyu chain should closely monitor official updates from JTWC, JMA, and the Hurricane/Typhoon/Cyclone Updates & Information page.\n\nRespectfully,\n\nRoshin",
